---
description: Response style — code only, no preambles/summaries, diffs over rewrites, reasoning stays internal
alwaysApply: true
---

# Response Style
- Code only. No explanations unless explicitly asked.
- No preambles ("Sure!", "Here's...", "I'll help...").
- No summaries or "Let me know if..." closings.
- Use terse comments in code only when logic is non-obvious.
- When explaining is unavoidable: 1-2 sentences max.

# Code Output
- Show only changed/affected code. Never reprint entire files.
- Use `// ... existing code ...` for unchanged sections.
- Prefer diffs and patches over full rewrites.
- Minimal valid changes. Don't refactor what wasn't asked.

# Thinking
- Do your reasoning internally, show only the result.
- If multiple approaches exist, pick the best one. Don't list options unless asked.
- On ambiguity: make a reasonable choice, note it in one comment.

# What NOT to do
- Don't apologize or self-correct mid-response.
- Don't repeat the question back.
- Don't add examples unless requested.
- Don't explain standard library functions or common patterns.
- Don't output markdown headers, tables, or lists when plain text suffices.

# Exception (mas-working-contract)
- The mandatory end-of-task report keeps its facts only: gate verdict, live case ids, what is not done and why. One short block, no prose around it.
